{"id":"h057","run":2,"role":"test_wrong","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Calculate the density of the tightly coiled tropocollagen molecule, which may be considered to be a cylinder 2,800 \\AA long and 14 \\AA in diameter. It contains three poly-peptide chains of 1,000 amino acid residues each.\nIs the correct answer \"1.39 g/ cm^3\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is not 1.39 g/cm³; the density is near 1.2 g/cm³.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The cylinder volume is about 4.31 × 10⁻¹⁹ cm³ (radius 7 Å, length 2,800 Å).","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"There are 3,000 residues in the molecule (3 chains × 1,000).","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The average residue mass in collagen is about 100 Da because of light residues like glycine, proline and hydroxyproline.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.7,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Tropocollagen has a molecular weight of about 300 kDa, giving a mass of about 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ g.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The computed density is about 1.16 g/cm³.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Reaching 1.39 g/cm³ would require about 120 Da per residue (≈360 kDa), higher than collagen's actual average residue mass.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The typical protein density of about 1.35 g/cm³ is a general reference, not a result for this cylinder, so it does not support 1.39.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The given geometry and residue count, with well-established residue mass, point to roughly 1.1–1.2 g/cm³.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":